    },
    {
      "id": "claim_dd53e2d60ed74399bacd0bb4ce505ed6",
      "statement": "The mock-backplane server distinguishes a modeled-but-absent fixture resource (HTTP 501) from a resource that exists but was never granted to the requesting trusted action (HTTP 403), so a gap in fixture coverage cannot be mistaken for a legitimate not-found or a passing negative test; kubectl cannot be used directly against the mock because it does not model Kubernetes API discovery endpoints.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://components/trusted-actions/tests/mock-backplane/README.md#L34-L80",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:712b62afead6fec4ea0cd84e20943a055b88c0dedde2673ee621ae0f05ea16f2:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_39ae2624886b49789e4adf65bf0ee924",
      "statement": "components/trusted-actions/tests/proxy-backplane/ is a recording/replay-style reverse proxy used by integration tests to reach a real Kubernetes API: it accepts a base64-encoded kubeconfig at registration (POST /backplane/trustedactions/{clusterId}), builds an http.Transport from its TLS credentials, and forwards arbitrary HTTP methods on the returned proxyUri to the live upstream cluster (injecting the kubeconfig's bearer token) until the registered entry is deleted; it runs via `make proxy-backplane` on PROXY_BACKPLANE_PORT (default 8088).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://components/trusted-actions/Makefile#L231-L234",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b213f7d68d5ba008e9d25726a18f7e31686e5049ce39bc842012b3fad0e0e618:"
        },
